  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Amelanchier alnifolia」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amelanchier_alnifolia",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
      "revision": 1376120571,
      "text": "Amelanchier alnifolia, the saskatoon berry, Pacific serviceberry, western serviceberry, western shadbush, or western juneberry, is a shrub native to North America. It is a member of the rose family, and bears an edible berry-like fruit."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Amelanchier alnifolia」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amelanchier_alnifolia#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
        "revision": 1376120571,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Seedlings are planted with 4.0–6.1 m (13–20 ft) between rows and 0.46–0.91 m (1.5–3 ft) between plants. An individual bush may bear fruit 30 or more years.\nSaskatoons are adaptable to most soil types with exception of poorly drained or heavy clay soils lacking organic matter. Shallow soils should be avoided, especially if the water table is high or erratic. Winter hardiness is exceptional, but frost can damage blooms as late as May. Large amounts of sunshine are needed for fruit ripening.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
